## 3. Function Descriptions

### Constants

- **`DEFAULT_CHAIN_PARAMS`** [C1] — `{ max_jump: 25 000, max_ooo_keys: 2 000 }`;
  the library-compiled defaults substituted for protobuf zeros.
- **`EPOCHS_TO_KEEP_PRIOR_TO_SEND_EPOCH` (1)** [C2] — Number of epochs
  retained *before* the send epoch when `send_key` advances epochs.
- **`KeyHistory::KEY_SIZE` (36)** [C3] — Byte size of one stored record:
  4-byte BE index ‖ 32-byte key. Extracted as `Result Std.Usize`
  (`4#usize + 32#usize`), so the spec first proves it evaluates to
  `ok 36#usize`.

### ChainParams / ChainParamsPB

- **`{impl Default}::default`** [F2] — Returns `DEFAULT_CHAIN_PARAMS`.
- **`ChainParams::into_pb`** [F3] — Maps each field to `0` iff it equals
  the default (protobuf zero-default convention), else keeps it.
  Spec: round-trip with F5/F6 getters.
- **`max_jump_or_default` / `max_ooo_keys_or_default`** [F5–F6] —
  `if v > 0 then v else default`; getter-with-default over the PB struct.
- **`trim_size`** [F7] — `max_ooo * 11 / 10 + 1` (as `usize`), the GC
  threshold. Requires `max_ooo < 390451572` for no-overflow
  (`hax_lib::assume!`, becomes a hypothesis); postconditions:
  `trim_size > max_ooo` and `trim_size ≥ 1`.

### KeyHistory

- **`KeyHistory::new`** [F8] — Empty history with capacity
  `KEY_SIZE * 2`. Spec: `historyModel data = []`, `HistWF` holds.
- **`KeyHistory::add(k, params)`** [F9] — Appends
  `k.0.to_be_bytes() ‖ k.1` to `data`. Spec:
  `historyModel data' = historyModel data ++ [(k.0, k.1)]`; preserves
  `HistWF` under the `requires` bound.
- **`KeyHistory::gc(current_key, params)`** [F10] — If
  `data.len() ≥ trim_size * KEY_SIZE`, one O(n) pass removing every
  record whose BE32 index is below `current_key − max_ooo` (the loop
  does **not** advance `i` after a removal, because `remove` swaps the
  last record in). Spec: multiset equality
  `historyModel data' = histGc (historyModel data)`.
- **`KeyHistory::clear`** [F11] — `data.clear()`; model becomes `[]`.
- **`KeyHistory::remove(i, params)`** [F12] — Swap-remove of the record
  at byte offset `i`: copies the last `KEY_SIZE` bytes onto position `i`
  (if not last) and truncates. Spec: model is a permutation of
  `historyModel data` with the record at `i / 36` deleted.
- **`KeyHistory::get(at, current_ctr, params)`** [F13] — Returns
  `Err(KeyTrimmed at)` if `at + max_ooo < current_ctr`; otherwise scans
  records in `KEY_SIZE` steps for BE32 index `= at`, returning the
  32-byte key and removing the record, else
  `Err(KeyAlreadyRequested at)`. Spec: lookup-and-delete on the model.

### ChainEpochDirection

- **`ChainEpochDirection::new(k)`** [F14] — `{ ctr := 0, next := k.to_vec(),
  prev := KeyHistory::new() }`.
- **`ChainEpochDirection::next_key`** [F15] — Wrapper around F16
  returning `(idx, key.to_vec())`; requires `next.len() > 0 ∧ ctr < u32::MAX`.
- **`ChainEpochDirection::next_key_internal(next, ctr)`** [F16] — The
  ratchet step: `ctr += 1`; HKDF(`next`, info = `ctr ‖ "Signal PQ
  Ratchet V1 Chain Next"`) into 64 bytes; the first 32 replace `next`,
  the last 32 are the returned key. Spec (relative to the KDF axiom):
  `ctr' = ctr + 1`, `next'.length = 32`, and `(next', key)` equals one
  step of `keyStream`.
- **`ChainEpochDirection::key(at, params)`** [F17] — Three-way compare
  of `at` with `ctr`: greater ⟹ jump check (`Err(KeyJump)` if beyond
  `max_jump`), then `clear` if the jump obsoletes all held keys, then
  the `key_loop` [L3] ratchets forward storing intermediate keys in
  `prev` (only those with `ctr + max_ooo ≥ at`), runs `gc`, and returns
  the final ratchet output; less ⟹ `prev.get`; equal ⟹
  `Err(KeyAlreadyRequested)`. This is the **hardest chain theorem**:
  it composes F9, F10, F11, F13, F16 and the loop invariant
  `ctr < u32::MAX ∧ cedInvariant`.
- **`ChainEpochDirection::into_pb` / `from_pb`** [F18–F19] — Field-wise
  record repackaging (`{ctr, next, prev.data}`); exact round-trip
  `from_pb (into_pb ced) = ok (Ok ced)` is provable with no axioms.
- **`ChainEpochDirection::clear_next`** [F20] — `next.clear()`; used by
  `send_key_loop1` to destroy send keys of superseded epochs
  (forward secrecy).

### Chain

- **`Chain::ced_for_direction(genr8r, dir)`** [F21] — Slices
  `genr8r[32..64]` (A2B) or `genr8r[64..96]` (B2A) as the initial key;
  requires `genr8r.len() = 96`.
- **`Chain::new(initial_key, dir, params)`** [F22] — HKDF-expands
  `initial_key` (info `"Signal PQ Ratchet V1 Chain  Start"`) into 96
  bytes: `next_root = [0..32]`, send/recv CEDs from F21 with `dir` and
  `dir.switch()`. Spec: `current_epoch = send_epoch = 0`,
  `links.length = 1`, and the A2B/B2A **direction symmetry**
  (`new k A2B`'s send CED = `new k B2A`'s recv CED).
- **`Chain::add_epoch(epoch_secret)`** [F23] — Asserts
  `epoch_secret.epoch = current_epoch + 1` (`massert` in Lean); ratchets
  `next_root` through HKDF (info `"Signal PQ Ratchet V1 Chain Add
  Epoch"`) and pushes a new `ChainEpoch` on `links`. Spec:
  `current_epoch' = epoch`, `links.length' = links.length + 1`.
- **`Chain::epoch_idx(epoch)`** [F24] — Affine index
  `links.len() − 1 − (current_epoch − epoch)` with two range checks
  (`Err(EpochOutOfRange)`). Spec: `Ok v → v < links.length` (mirrors
  the Rust `ensures`), plus the affine characterization `epochIdx_affine`.
- **`Chain::send_key(epoch)`** [F25] — Errors if
  `epoch < send_epoch`; if the send epoch advances, `send_key_loop0`
  [L4] pops old epochs down to `EPOCHS_TO_KEEP_PRIOR_TO_SEND_EPOCH`
  and `send_key_loop1` [L5] clears `send.next` of all remaining prior
  epochs; finally returns `links[idx].send.next_key()`. Composes F15,
  F20, F24 and the deque-reindexing lemma `epochIdx_pop_front`.
- **`Chain::recv_key(epoch, index)`** [F26] — `epoch_idx` +
  `links[idx].recv.key(index, params)`; delegates to F17 and F24 via
  the `ControlFlow` branch of the extracted `?` operator.
- **`Chain::into_pb`** [F27] — `#[hax_lib::opaque]` in Rust for hax,
  but **extracted by Aeneas** with an explicit closure [F28]
  (`FnMut`/`FnOnce` instances mapping each `ChainEpoch` to a PB epoch
  via F18) and `map`/`collect` scaffolding.
- **`Chain::into_pb::closure`** [F28] — The per-link PB constructor
  `{ send := some (into_pb l.send), recv := some (into_pb l.recv) }`.
- **`Chain::from_pb`** [F29] — Inverse of F27: direction `try_into`,
  per-link F19 (with `ok_or(StateDecode)` on missing fields, closures
  F30/F31), `params.ok_or(StateDecode)`. Target theorem: PB round-trip
  `from_pb (into_pb c) = ok (Ok c)`.
- **`Chain::from_pb::closure` / `closure#1`** [F30–F31] — The
  `ok_or(Error::StateDecode)` mapper and the per-link `ChainEpoch`
  reconstructor, respectively (extracted as `Unit` closure states with
  `Fn*` instances).

### Test Functions (not extracted)

- **F32–F38** — Unit tests and proptests for direction symmetry, key
  replay rejection, trimming, out-of-order delivery, send-key erasure,
  and the two model-based property tests. Not extracted by Aeneas.

